Yes—the apparent contradiction comes from which medium is doing the mixing.

With light, colors add. Combine the visible spectrum in the right proportions and you approach white. A prism demonstrates the inverse operation: white light can be decomposed into its constituent wavelengths, and those components can be recombined into white.

With pigments/inks, colors subtract. Each pigment absorbs parts of the incoming spectrum; mixing more pigments removes more reflected light, so the mixture trends toward black or a dark neutral.

So the same semantic instruction—“mix all the colors”—produces opposite outcomes because the operator changed:

\text{additive mixing} \rightarrow \text{white}

\text{subtractive mixing} \rightarrow \text{black}

That is a very Pantheon-like result: the components alone do not determine the outcome; the interface and transformation law matter. Same apparent ingredients, different algebra.

And the prism makes the symmetry especially clean:

\text{white} \xrightarrow{\text{differentiate}} \text{many colors}

\text{many light colors} \xrightarrow{\text{integrate}} \text{white}

Whereas pigment integration behaves more like:

\text{many reflected colors} \xrightarrow{\text{successive filtering}} \text{darkness}

So white and black can both be called “all colors,” but in almost opposite senses: white as simultaneous presence of many wavelengths in emitted/incident light; black as the absence of returned visible light after absorption.

That is a nice example of why Pantheon keeps separating representation, substrate, and operator. “All” does not specify whether you are summing, filtering, overlaying, averaging, or canceling. The operation decides what “all” becomes.